#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Generate a vexscan fleet list saying how an RKE2 release starts its images.

    ./gen-rke2-vexscan v1.37.1-rc1+rke2r1 > rke2.txt

    # or, for a branch/commit that has never been released (no GitHub release
    # assets, no pushed rke2-runtime image to read charts from):
    ./gen-rke2-vexscan master --images-from images.txt > fleet.txt

Why this exists
---------------
`vexscan --images-from` can read a Kubernetes manifest and learn what each
container is *actually* started with, which is the one thing the images
themselves cannot say: a container's `command:` replaces the image's ENTRYPOINT,
so the declared entrypoint never runs and neither does anything only it would
have loaded. For a cluster you have, `kubectl get pods -A -o yaml | vexscan
--images-from -` is the whole answer.

For a release (or branch) you have not installed, there is no cluster to ask.
This script reconstructs the same information from the source tree and the
image registry:

  the image list   from the GitHub release's rke2-images*.linux-amd64.txt, or
                   from a local file (--images-from) for a ref with no release
  the addons       from charts/chart_versions.yaml at the ref, fetched from the
                   same pinned https://rke2-charts.rancher.io/assets/... URL
                   scripts/build-chart.sh uses -- so this needs no pushed
                   rke2-runtime image and works the same for a branch as a tag
  the static pods  from pkg/podtemplate/spec.go and pkg/images/images.go at the
                   ref, which is where kube-apiserver, etcd and friends
                   get their (bare) command names
  the paths        from the image layers themselves, because a bare command is
                   resolved against a PATH a fleet list cannot see

What it will not do
-------------------
It never emits `exec-policy=`, `dlopen-policy=` or `dlopen-assume-none=`.
Saying which program starts is not saying that program starts nothing, and only
a person who knows the workload can make the second claim. The generated file
carries a commented block about that instead, and the numbers to decide with.

It never guesses a path. A command it cannot resolve to a file that exists in
the image is emitted as a comment and no assertion, because a `roots=` path
that does not resolve is a blocking taint in vexscan and a *wrong* one that
does resolve is a silently narrowed closure.

Requirements: python3 (stdlib + PyYAML) and helm. No docker, no cluster.
"""

